Last time I said I'd write again once the Claude layer was designed. Well, it got
designed, then built, and then Phase 5 came along and turned the whole thing into an
actual website you can use. That was a busy few days. The short version is that you
can now paste a 17Lands link and get a full review of your draft back, and there's a
wizard involved.

First, the question I left hanging: what does a review say when you didn't make any
mistakes? The answer I landed on is a **Noted Pick**. Any `questionable` or `mistake`
pick gets a written note, and so does a "narrow" pick, where the pack had exactly one
right answer hiding among a couple of tempting wrong ones and you found it. Everything
else just gets its verdict, no essay attached. A draft with nothing worth noting is a
perfectly valid outcome, and I'd rather say nothing than pad it out.

## Paste a link, get a review

The core loop is done. You hand the site a 17Lands link, the backend fetches that
draft once and keeps the parsed log in what I'm calling the Replay Cache, and every
verdict gets recomputed from it on the spot. Nothing about the review itself is
stored. If the card stats update overnight, your review updates with them, and a
second look at the same draft never bothers 17Lands again.

I also threw out a whole job queue from the original plan. It assumed reviews would
take 10 to 20 seconds and need background workers and polling. In reality the verdicts
are pure Python and come back almost instantly, so every request just waits for its
answer like a normal web page. One less moving part to break.

## Building your deck

After the picks, there's a deck builder: drag your 40 cards in and get Deck Ratings
back. Each part of the deck (card quality, curve, synergy, removal, mana) gets a letter
grade based on where it ranks against real 40-card decks from the 17Lands game data.
So a B in curve means your curve stacks up well against the decks people actually
played, not that I decided 16 creatures sounded like a B.

The overall grade comes from a regression of game wins on those same metrics, fitted
per format, which means the data decides which parts of a deck matter most. For the
mana, I compare your color sources against Frank Karsten's published tables for
casting spells on curve, because that question already has a well-known answer and I
had no business reinventing it.

## Claude, behind a locked door

Pick Notes are written by Claude Haiku the moment you open one, and the longer Draft
Summary comes from Claude Sonnet after you submit a deck. Both are fully built and
running on the live site, but only for me for now, behind an admin key. Accounts don't
exist until Phase 6, and I'm not letting anonymous traffic run up a Claude bill. When
accounts land, the key check just becomes a signed-in check and nothing else changes.

## Meet Gnarl

The first version of the review page was a deliberate placeholder, and it looked like
one. The overhaul gives the whole site a library feel: parchment backgrounds, dark
blue ink, lapis for links, and EB Garamond for the headings. It also gives the coach a
face. His name is **Gnarl Edgibel**, he's a little cartoon wizard in a periwinkle robe,
and he has no mouth, which I find really charming. The art is commissioned, and for now
he has exactly one pose, pointing, which doubles as his "explaining" pose.

Gnarl only shows up where Claude is talking. His notes appear in a Dialogue Box, with
Gnarl framed in a book spine next to a parchment panel holding the text. He never
appears next to the numbers, because the numbers are Python's, not his. Every card in a
pack also got its own Pack Card with two borders: the inner one shows the verdict color,
and the outer one shows whether it was your pick.

## A Draft Summary with something to say

The first Draft Summary was about 200 words, and reading real ones, I didn't love
them. They said what happened without saying why, and they skipped whole topics, like
how the deck actually wins or what was sitting in the sideboard. Since the output
tokens are cheap next to the brief going in, tripling the length only costs about a
cent. So version two is about 640 words in seven sections: how the draft went, the
signals, the deck, how it plays, the build, a short numbered list of lessons, and a
couple of key picks.

It also gets a lot more to work with. Python now tracks every time your draft really
changed colors, counting a switch only once the new pair holds for three picks in a
row so one stray pick doesn't register as a pivot. It also scores your sideboard
against the deck you built and passes along any cards that were better than your
weakest playable. I ran twelve test summaries through the live eval, all twelve passed
the "no invented numbers" check, each took 11 to 15 seconds, and the whole run cost
about 53 cents.

## Tomes

Last up, the system for actually handing out Claude calls. Unlocking a draft's
Gnarl's Notes costs one **Tome**, and once a draft is unlocked, all of its notes and its
summary are free. Everyone gets two tomes a day, reset at midnight UTC, and they don't
stack. There's no stored balance anywhere either. The site just counts how many drafts
you've unlocked since midnight, which leaves one less number that can drift out of sync.
For now only I can see the button, but it's ready for when accounts arrive.

What's left in Phase 5 is a few smaller coaching tweaks and then verification, which
means running my own drafts through it, ones where I already know what I did wrong. If
BoosterTutor doesn't catch the mistake, that's a bug in the analysis, not in the prompt.
